            component.
          %h3#as_a_free_category_with_finite_products As a free category with finite products
          %p
            A cartesian multicategory can also be defined as a category with specified
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+products"} finite products
            whose set of objects under the “product” operation is a free monoid on specified generators.
          %p
            When there is exactly one such generator, this recovers the definition of a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
          %h3#alternative_presentation Alternative presentation
          %p
            The composition structure of a cartesian multicategory can also be equivalently presented in the style usually used for abstract clones. The symmetry, contraction and weakening are derivable rather than required. In this style, a cartesian multicategory is given by a collection of objects, and for every sequence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mi …
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1\dots c_{n+1}
            of objects, a collection of multimorphisms
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mn 1
                    %mi …
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mi n
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo ;
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi c
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom(c_1\dots c_{n}\;;\;c_{n+1})
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                for every sequence
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 1
                      %mi …
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_{1}\dots c_{n}
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo ≤
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i\leq n
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mi i
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 1
                      %mi …
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi n
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ;
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{i} \in hom(c_{1} \dots c_{n} \;;\; c_{i})
            %li
              %p
                for sequences
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi c
                          %mn 1
                        %mi …
                        %msub
                          %mi c
                          %mi m
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1\dots c_m
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mn 1
                      %mi …
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_1\dots d_{n}
                and object
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi e
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∘
                      %mo :
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mn 1
                      %mi …
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi n
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ;
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi e
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ×
                      %munderover
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mo =
                          %mn 1
                        %mi n
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 1
                      %mi …
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi m
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo ;
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %msub
                        %mi d
                        %mi i
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi hom
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mn 1
                      %mi …
                      %msub
                        %mi c
                        %mi m
                      %mo ,
                      %mi e
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ : hom(d_1\dots d_{n} \;;\; e)\times \prod_{i=1}^{n} hom(c_1\dots c_m \;;\; d_i)\to hom(c_1\dots c_m,e)
          %p all such that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                identity laws:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo ∘
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi π
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\circ (\pi_i)_i = f
                and
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi π
                          %mi i
                        %mo ∘
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mn 0
                        %mi …
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi f
                          %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_i \circ (f_0\dots f_{n-1})=f_i
            %li
              %p
                associativity law:
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo ∘
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi g
                          %mn 1
                        %mo ∘
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi h
                          %mn 1
                        %mi …
                        %msub
                          %mi h
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi …
                        %msub
                          %mi g
                          %mi m
                        %mo ∘
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi h
                          %mn 1
                        %mi …
                        %msub
                          %mi h
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi f
                        %mo ∘
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi g
                          %mn 1
                        %mi …
                        %msub
                          %mi g
                          %mi m
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ∘
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi h
                          %mn 1
                        %mi …
                        %msub
                          %mi h
                          %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\circ (g_1\circ (h_1\dots h_{n})\dots g_{m}\circ (h_1\dots h_{n})) = f \circ (g_1\dots g_{m}) \circ (h_1\dots h_{n})
          %h2#representability Representability
          %p
            A cartesian multicategory, like an ordinary multicategory, is
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+multicategory"} representable
            if for any finite list
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (c_1,\dots,c_n)
            of objects there exists an object “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1 \times \cdots \times c_n
            ” and a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c_1,\dots,c_n \to c_1 \times \cdots \times c_n
            which is universal, in that the following induced functions are all bijections:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi k
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ×
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi m
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ;
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi d
                    %mi k
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi c
                    %mi n
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi m
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ;
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  hom(d_1,\dots,d_k, c_1 \times \cdots \times c_n , e_1,\dots,e_m \;;\; f) \to
                  hom(d_1,\dots,d_k, c_1,\dots,c_n, e_1,\dots,e_m \;;\; f)
          %p
            Just as representable (symmetric) multicategories are equivalent to (symmetric) monoidal categories, representable cartesian multicategories are equivalent to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+monoidal+categories"} cartesian monoidal categories
          %p
            More abstractly, representable cartesian multicategories are the algebras for a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colax-idempotent+2-monad"} colax-idempotent 2-monad
            on the 2-category of cartesian multicategories, whose algebras are categories with finite products.
          %h2#syntax_semantics_and_type_theory Syntax, semantics, and type theory
          %p
            Small cartesian multicategories may be considered presentations of “many-sorted finite-product theories”. On the spectrum between syntax and semantics, they sit in between a fully syntactic presentation (such as a
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+structure"} free category
            with finite products generated by a model of some theory).
          %p
            Cartesian multicategories are also a natural place to talk about the semantics of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contraction+rule"} contraction
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weakening+rule"} weakening
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"} dependent types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contexts"} contexts
            in order to recapture all the terms. This usual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+contexts"} category of contexts
            can be recaptured as the free category-with-finite-products generated by this cartesian multicategory of types. Similarly, we can talk about models of such a type theory in any cartesian multicategory.
          %h2#related_pages Related pages
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategory"} multicategory
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+multicategory"} symmetric multicategory
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+multicategory"} generalized multicategory
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere+theory"} Lawvere theory
            %li
              A cartesian multicategory with one object is equivalent to an
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abstract+clone"} abstract clone
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+multicategory"} sequential multicategory
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Cartesian multicategories were introduced under the name
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerhard+Gentzen"} Gentzen
              multicategories
            in §10 of:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joachim+Lambek"}> Joachim Lambek
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Multicategories revisited
          %p
            They are called
            %strong finite product multicategories
            in the following reference. The author proves that cartesian operads are monadic over symmetric operads, which are monadic over operads, which are monadic over
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Miles Gould,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Coherence for categorified operadic theories
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claudio+Pisani"}> Claudio Pisani
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Sequential multicategories
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1402.0253"} arXiv:1402.0253
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nathanael+Arkor"} Nathanael Arkor
                and
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dylan+McDermott"} Dylan McDermott
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Abstract Clones for Abstract Syntax
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.00969"} arXiv
            %li#Pisani25
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Claudio+Pisani"}> Claudio Pisani
                , “Unbiased multicategory theory”, Theory and Applications of Categories, Vol. 44, 2025, No. 28, pp 826-868.
                %a{:href => "http://tac.mta.ca/tac/volumes/44/28/44-28abs.html"} web
